          "callID": "call_8dd7963d060a4296896c45ea",
          "state": {
            "status": "completed",
            "input": {
              "query": "\"Density is reproduced to within 4%\" \"seven of the eight molecules\" Ogbomo thermal conductivity",
              "type": "deep"
            },
            "output": "{\n  \"search_id\": \"search_fda4d4db881c8fe966de7082d52024f1\",\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13647254\",\n      \"title\": \"Benchmarking machine learning and molecular dynamics methods for the prediction of the thermophysical properties of organic liquids\",\n      \"publish_date\": \"2026-09-29\",\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"Accurate prediction of thermophysical properties is crucial for the rational design of lubricants and cooling fluids. We compile a database of approximately 4700 critically evaluated reference values for the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of 835 unique organic molecules in the C10–C50 range.\\nEquilibrium molecular dynamics (EMD) simulations of eight representative molecules reproduce density accurately but show systematic, chemistry-dependent force-field errors for the viscosity and thermal conductivity.\\nToc\\nMachine learning models trained on ∼4700 reference values predict the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of lubricant-sized organic molecules, benchmarked against Green–Kubo molecular dynamics simulations of eight representative fluids.\\nIntroduction\\nAll the MD simulations were performed using the LAMMPS software.^102 EMD simulations were employed to calculate the density, viscosity, and thermal conductivity of the eight target molecules at 40 °C and 100 °C, consistent with conditions used to determine lubricant viscosity index values.^8 All simulations were performed using the LOPLS-AA force\\nOf the three properties, density is reproduced most accurately. MD matches the reference values to within 3% for six of the eight molecules at 40 °C (within 4% for seven) and within 6% for seven of the eight at 100 °C (Fig.\\n7a and S10a), consistent with previous benchmarks for linear and branched alkanes.^26 The instantaneous density reaches a stable plateau within approximately 0.2 ns of the 1 ns NPT equilibration (shown for all eight molecules at both temperatures in Fig. S11), and no residual equilibration drift is detectable in the reported values.\\nFour of the eight molecules lie within 25% of the reference values at 40 °C, with squalane under-predicted by 42% and decyl stearate, 4-hexyl-1,7-diphenylheptane, and bis(2-ethylhexyl) sebacate over-predicted by 44, 69, and 137%; at 100 °C four lie within 10%, with decyl stearate (+45%), 7,10,13-trioxanonadecane (−27%), tetraethylene glycol\\nClassical force fields are parameterized primarily for equilibrium thermodynamics, above all liquid density, which they reproduce here to within 4% for seven of eight molecules. On the other hand, these force fields are not fit to transport properties.\\nThe MD benchmark, using a Green–Kubo protocol with quantified trajectory-length convergence, shows that the accuracy of the LOPLS-AA force field is property- and chemistry-dependent. Density is reproduced to within 4% for seven of the eight molecules at 40 °C.\\nThe ML models, trained directly on the target properties, show no comparable bias across the chemical families of this eight-molecule case study: their OOF predictions stay close to the reference values for the density and thermal conductivity of these molecules, with larger but unsystematic errors for viscosity, at an inference cost some seven\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/jres/087/jresv87n4p279_A1b.pdf\",\n      \"title\": \"The Thermal Conductivity of Oxygen - NIST\",\n      \"publish_date\": null,\n      \"excerpts\": [\n        \"The Thermal Conductivity of Oxygen - NIST\\nnew experimental measurements are presented that cover a large range in density for every isotherm, i.e., 0 to 19 mol/L for 310 K and 0 to 40 mol/L for 78 K.\\nThe new results and a theoretical calculation for the dilute gas [6,7] are used to fashion a new correlation for the thermal conductivity surface of oxygen between 78 and 310 K for\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"url\": \"https://nvlpubs.nis